{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791215257,"nonce":"rVlm/xIOKAqA39oXtCFcOMTAlNE=","title":"Comprehension over an iterator call types its variable Dynamic","message":"var counts = new Map\u003cString, Int\u003e(); counts.set(\"AA\", 3);\n    var keys = [for (key in counts.keys()) key];\n    trace(keys[0]);          // \u003cunknown type 1148865816\u003e\n    var k:String = keys[0];  // null\n\nThe comprehension's loop variable is typed by `infer_iterator_types`, which knows Array/Map/String/IntIterator and falls back to Dynamic for anything else, including `map.keys()`. The value is a raw string, so the Dynamic-typed array element is later read as a box. A `for` statement over the same iterable uses `infer_element_type_from_iterable` (iterator calls, structural iterables) and gets String. `var keys:Array\u003cString\u003e = [...]` worked because the annotation retyped it.\n\nFound in the knucleotide benchmark kernel (sorted keys printed as \u003cunknown type\u003e).\n\nFix: both comprehension forms fall back to the statement's inference when the old one yields Dynamic. Nuc A/B (base c8147a87 vs fix): conformance 690 -\u003e 690, no row moved; Haxe suite 207/0/1 both (one test_exception SIGSEGV in a suite run of the fix arm did not reproduce in 8 reruns); compiler integration 830 passed both; lib tests 742 passed; clippy clean.","files":null}]}